From 1f6b52142384d82db36a75b6a4194bd1f5955129 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 08:47:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Emit a generic fallback inside the specialized method, item
 4-3b

The transfer 4-3's design spike identified as the one restart cannot give:
compile the specialized and generic forms into ONE method and make a failed
guard a BRANCH. The CLR locals are shared because it is the same method, so no
transfer exists to get wrong; nothing is re-entered, so effects already
performed are never repeated; and no CallFrameStack slot changes hands, so the
three invariants 4-3a preserves are not engaged at all. That is what makes
speculation legal after effects have begun, which is what 4-2 and 4-4 need.

SpeculationBuilder.Guarded emits it; Runtime/Speculation.cs carries the site
table. Purely additive -- no existing file changes.

The guarantee that justifies a facility rather than a hand-rolled conditional:
the subject is evaluated EXACTLY ONCE, into a temporary the guard and both arms
share. The obvious hand-rolled spelling evaluates it in the guard and again in
whichever arm runs, so a receiver with an effect (f().x) would run f() twice --
a wrong answer visible only on effectful receivers. Substituting that spelling
fails 12 of the 15 tests, which is how the tests are known to see it.

Poisoning is part of the mechanism. A guard that keeps failing costs its own
evaluation on every execution plus the generic path, which is worse than never
speculating; after four misses (the threshold the inline cache and 4-1 also
use) the site short-circuits to generic. Deliberately a stand-in: the right
answer once 4-2 exists is to re-emit the method without the guard, since a
poisoned site still pays one static array read here.

No JavaScript-level speculation is emitted, and that is structural rather than
scope-trimming. A guard needs something to speculate on -- a shape, a callee, a
numeric type -- and in a tier-1 method compiled before anything has run, none
of those is known; that is why 4-1 exists. So the in-method branch only has
meaning inside a tier-2 recompile, and tier-2 emission is item 4-2. The
mechanism has to land before its first consumer, and its first consumer is the
next item.

Adds InMethodFallbackTests (15 cases), built as expression trees and compiled
through the engine's own IL generator because testing this through JavaScript
would test whatever chose to emit it instead. The centre of the file is the
phase's own stated verification: bodies of 1, 2, 3 and 5 guarded operations
with effects before, between and after them, every guard failed, compared
against an unspeculated control compiled from the same shape -- the effect logs
must match entry for entry. Then each guard is failed individually while the
rest hold, asserting every prior effect happened exactly once and only the
failing operation took the generic path.
